{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Positional Notation.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Positional Notation.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"positional notation positional notation","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Positional Notation.md","fileName":"Positional Notation.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Positional Notation","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-05 23:41\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Number Systems and Arithmetic]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Number Systems and Arithmetic","algebraessentials.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Positional Notation.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Positional Notation.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"positional notation positional notation","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Positional Notation.md","fileName":"Positional Notation.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Positional Notation","heading":"Positional Notation","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Positional notation is a numeral system in which a symbol's contribution depends on its location. Adjacent positions differ by a constant multiplier called the base, and each position represents a power of that base.\n\nIn base ten,\n\n$$1234=1\\cdot10^3+2\\cdot10^2+3\\cdot10+4.$$\n\nThe same digit therefore denotes different quantities in different positions. Positional notation makes arithmetic far more compact than a purely additive sign-value system and explains how a finite collection of digit symbols can represent arbitrarily large numbers and decimal fractions.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Number Systems and Arithmetic","algebraessentials.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Positional Notation.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Positional Notation.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey"